{
  "image_name": "84ebbaf39e5e3e8b5a3863ffb247458f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several mineral-driven pathways by which organic carbon is oxidized at mineral surfaces, emphasizing electron transfer and reactive oxygen species. Across the schemes, reduced organic carbon donates electrons to minerals or mineral-associated metals, and the organics become oxidized.\n\nOne route shows direct coupling on birnessite, where electrons move from organic carbon into the layered manganese oxide structure, changing the mineral’s redox state. A second route involves goethite: aqueous Fe²⁺ released from the mineral appears to shuttle electrons and react with oxygen, generating species such as superoxide, hydroxyl radical, and hydrogen peroxide that then promote organic oxidation. Another pathway highlights structural or hosted Fe(II) in minerals such as nontronite, again linking electron transfer to oxygen reduction and superoxide formation.\n\nThe figure also stresses that defects in birnessite can act as reactive sites where oxygen accepts electrons, and that light can drive similar chemistry on rutile (TiO₂), producing oxygen radicals photochemically. A legend identifies octahedral and tetrahedral metal centers, microbes, and arrows for electron transfer versus transport. Overall, the image compares direct mineral oxidation, ROS-mediated oxidation, defect-driven reactions, and photocatalytic pathways as related mechanisms for organic matter transformation.",
